# Grade 8: teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'8-1-1': dict(
  saint="Clement of Rome: the pope who wrote to Corinth and showed that the apostles’ authority was handed on.",
  tryit=["Trace the line from Jesus to the apostles to their successors.", "Mark where Peter appears.", "Say what the keys mean."],
  saintStory="Clement was the fourth bishop of Rome, about AD 88 to 97, after Peter, Linus and Cletus. Around the year 96, the Christians in Corinth removed some of their appointed leaders, and the community split. Clement wrote them a letter on behalf of the Church in Rome, which is the oldest Christian writing outside the New Testament.\nIn it, he wrote that the apostles had appointed bishops and deacons, and had arranged for others to succeed them when they died, so that the order would continue. He urged the Corinthians to restore peace and humility. “Why are there strife and anger and division among you?” Remarkably, the bishop of Rome intervened in a church far away, and it was received with respect. The letter was read in Corinth at Mass for generations.\nTradition says Clement was exiled to the Crimea, and martyred. He is named in the Roman Canon of the Mass.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write on the board, “Whom did Jesus leave in charge?” Take answers. Then ask, “How do we know?”",
    teach=["Read Matthew 16:13-19. Say: “At Caesarea Philippi, Jesus asked, ‘Who do you say that I am?’ Simon Peter answered, ‘You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.’ Jesus said, ‘Blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jonah… you are Peter, and on this rock I will build my church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it. I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven.’ Jesus gave Simon a new name, Rock (Cephas in Aramaic), and the keys, which in Isaiah 22 belonged to the king’s steward, the chief official. Peter has a special authority.”", "Say: “Jesus also chose twelve apostles and gave them authority: ‘Whoever listens to you listens to me.’ (Luke 10:16) He sent them: ‘Go therefore and make disciples of all nations.’ (Matthew 28:19) After the Resurrection he gave them the power to forgive sins (John 20:23). He promised the Holy Spirit to lead them into all truth. And to Peter: ‘Feed my sheep.’ (John 21:17) The Church is not a human club but a community founded by Christ on the apostles, with Peter at the head.”", "Say: “The apostles had to hand on their ministry. In Acts 1, Matthias replaced Judas. Paul named Timothy and Titus to appoint elders. This is called apostolic succession: the bishops are the successors of the apostles, and the bishop of Rome, the Pope, is the successor of Peter. St. Clement’s letter shows this was understood in the first century. The Catholic Church is the one that can trace its bishops back, in an unbroken line, to the apostles. This gives us confidence that the teaching is the teaching handed on by Christ.”"],
    ask=["What did Jesus give Peter? (A new name, the keys, authority to bind and loose.)", "What is apostolic succession? (Bishops succeeding the apostles in an unbroken line.)", "Who is Peter’s successor today? (The Pope, the bishop of Rome.)"],
    practice=["Draw a vertical chain: Jesus, Peter and the apostles, their successors, the bishops today.", "Mark the key Bible verses beside each link (Matthew 16:18; Luke 10:16; John 21:17; 2 Timothy 2:2).", "Add the name of your own bishop and the current Pope at the bottom.", "Explain the chain to a partner."],
    close="Pray for the Pope and your bishop. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Find out the name of your bishop.”")),

'8-1-2': dict(
  saint="Catherine of Siena: a lay woman who loved the Church so much that she told the pope to come home.",
  tryit=["Match the images of the Church to Scripture.", "Explain what each shows.", "Draw one."],
  saintStory="Catherine Benincasa was born in 1347 in Siena, Italy, the twenty-fourth of twenty-five children. At six or seven she had a vision of Christ, and decided to give her life to him. Her family wanted her to marry. She cut off her hair in protest, and finally they allowed her to become a lay Dominican, living at home in prayer.\nShe spent hours praying in a small room, and then began to nurse the sick, including plague victims and a man with leprosy. People were drawn to her. She started writing letters to everyone: popes, kings and ordinary people, all in a direct and loving voice. She did not know how to write, and dictated them at first.\nAt that time, the popes lived in Avignon, in France, rather than Rome. Catherine wrote to Pope Gregory XI, urging him to return. In 1377 he did. She also spent her last years trying to heal the Church from the Western Schism. She died in 1380, at thirty-three, and is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Draw a stick-figure body on the board and ask students to label the parts with jobs in a parish: hands, eyes, feet. Say, “What happens when one part stops working?” Use their answers to open Paul’s image.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is a mystery. That means she is not only a human organization, but a reality with a divine side that we cannot fully grasp. The Catechism says the Church is ‘visible and spiritual,’ a human community and the presence of Christ. Scripture gives us images of the Church. The People of God: God’s chosen people, now made up of all nations (1 Peter 2:9). The Body of Christ: Christ is the head and we are the members (1 Corinthians 12). The Temple of the Holy Spirit: ‘Do you not know that you are God’s temple?’ (1 Corinthians 3:16). The Bride of Christ: Christ loves her (Ephesians 5). The Flock: Christ is the Good Shepherd (John 10). The Vine and branches: ‘I am the vine, you are the branches.’ (John 15)”", "Say: “The Church is also called a sacrament: ‘a sign and instrument of communion with God and of unity among all men.’ (Lumen Gentium 1) She is the visible sign through which Christ is present to the world and gives grace. She is not perfect. The Church is made up of sinners, and always needs reform. Catherine of Siena saw this, and loved the Church even more. The Church is called holy because Christ is holy, and because the Holy Spirit works in her, even though her members sin.”", "Say: “Paul’s image of the Body is the most powerful. ‘Now you are the body of Christ and individually members of it.’ If you are baptized, you are a member. Each member has a role. When one suffers, all suffer. Christ lives in the Church; when Saul persecuted Christians, Jesus said, ‘Why do you persecute me?’ This is why we say the Church is not optional. To love Christ is to love his body. St. Cyprian said, ‘No one can have God for his Father who does not have the Church for his mother.’”"],
    ask=["Name three images of the Church. (People of God, Body of Christ, Temple of the Spirit, Bride, Flock.)", "What does it mean that the Church is a sacrament? (A visible sign and instrument of God’s presence.)", "How can the Church be holy and have sinners? (Christ is holy; the Spirit works in her.)"],
    practice=["Divide into groups. Each takes an image: People of God, Body of Christ, Temple of the Spirit, Bride, Flock, Vine.", "Draw it, and write the Scripture verse and what it shows about the Church.", "Present to the class.", "End by writing: “Being a member of the Church means that I…”"],
    close="Pray for the Church: “Lord, make your Church holy.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask an adult what they love about the Church.”")),

'8-1-3': dict(
  saint="Ignatius of Antioch: the bishop who first used the words “Catholic Church” and was eaten by lions.",
  tryit=["Define each mark in a sentence.", "Find a sign of each in your parish.", "Test: could another community claim it?"],
  saintStory="Ignatius was bishop of Antioch in Syria around the year 100, probably a disciple of the apostle John. He was arrested during the reign of Trajan and sent as a prisoner to Rome to be killed by wild beasts. On the long journey, guarded by soldiers he called “ten leopards,” he wrote seven letters to churches along the way, and to Polycarp.\nIn them, he wrote about the unity of the Church around the bishop, “Where the bishop appears, there let the people be, just as where Christ Jesus is, there is the Catholic Church.” This is the earliest known use of the term “Catholic Church.” He also wrote of the Eucharist as “the medicine of immortality” and the flesh of our Savior Jesus Christ.\nHe begged the Christians in Rome not to rescue him: “I am the wheat of God, and I am ground by the teeth of wild beasts, that I may be found the pure bread of Christ.” He died in about 107.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How would you tell a real $20 bill from a fake? What marks do you check?” Take answers. “How could we tell the real Church of Christ? The Creed gives four marks.”",
    teach=["Say: “In the Nicene Creed, we say, ‘I believe in one, holy, catholic and apostolic Church.’ These are the four marks of the Church. One: the Church is one because her source is the Trinity, with one Lord, one faith, one Baptism (Ephesians 4:4-6). She is united in faith, sacraments and governance under the Pope and bishops. Christians are divided, and Jesus prayed ‘that they may all be one.’ (John 17:21) We work for unity, and pray for it.”", "Say: “Holy: the Church is holy because Christ is holy, because he gave her the means of holiness (the sacraments, the Word) and because she produces saints. This does not mean that members are sinless. Catholic: the word means ‘universal’ or ‘according to the whole.’ She has the fullness of the faith and the means of salvation, and she is for all peoples, in all times and places. Apostolic: she is founded on the apostles, whose teaching she keeps, and whose authority is handed on through the bishops.”", "Say: “These marks can be seen. There is one faith across the world: the same Creed, the same seven sacraments, the same Mass readings in every language. There is a Pope and bishops, tracing back to the apostles. There are saints in every age and country. Other Christian communities have real elements of truth and holiness, and we respect them; we do not say they have nothing. The Catholic Church teaches that the fullness of the means of salvation subsists in her. As Ignatius said, where Christ is, there is the Catholic Church.”"],
    ask=["Name the four marks. (One, holy, catholic, apostolic.)", "What does ‘catholic’ mean? (Universal; the fullness of the faith for all.)", "What do we do about divisions among Christians? (Pray and work for unity.)"],
    practice=["Make four columns: One, Holy, Catholic, Apostolic.", "Under each, write a definition, a Bible verse, and one thing you can see in your own parish or diocese.", "Add a test: What would a community lack if it were missing this mark?", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray the Creed slowly, especially the line on the Church. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for Christian unity.”")),

'8-2-1': dict(
  saint="Perpetua and Felicity: two young mothers who died in the arena at Carthage in 203.",
  tryit=["Read a martyr’s own words.", "Answer: what did she refuse, and why?", "Say what the Church gained."],
  saintStory="Vibia Perpetua was a noblewoman of about twenty-two in Carthage, in North Africa, with an infant son. Felicity was her slave, eight months pregnant. They were arrested in 203 with others for being Christian catechumens, under an order against conversion. Perpetua kept a diary in prison, one of the earliest writings by a Christian woman.\nHer father, a pagan, begged her to offer incense and save herself. “Father, do you see this vessel? Can it be called by any other name than what it is?” “No.” “Neither can I call myself anything else than what I am, a Christian.” She and her companions were baptized in prison. Felicity gave birth to a daughter just days before, and a Christian woman adopted the baby.\nOn March 7, 203, they were sent into the arena. Wounded by a wild cow, Perpetua asked for a pin to fasten her torn tunic, out of modesty. They gave each other the kiss of peace, and were killed by the sword. Their names are in the Roman Canon.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud a short excerpt from the letters of Pliny to Trajan about Christians. Ask, “What did the governor find? What surprised him about these people?” Take notes on the board.",
    teach=["Say: “For about three hundred years, Christians were persecuted by the Roman Empire. Nero blamed them for the fire in Rome in AD 64. Peter and Paul were killed. In AD 250, Emperor Decius ordered everyone to offer sacrifice to the Roman gods. Diocletian began the Great Persecution in 303, which was the worst. Christians were refused because they would not worship the emperor or the gods; because they met in secret; and because they were thought to be atheists, since they had no idols. Christians were arrested, tortured, put in arenas against wild beasts, burned or beheaded.”", "Say: “The word ‘martyr’ means ‘witness.’ A martyr dies for the faith, because of hatred of Christ. The Church honors martyrs as having the greatest love (John 15:13). Stephen, killed in Acts 7, was the first. Ignatius of Antioch, Polycarp of Smyrna, Justin, Agnes, Lawrence, Cecilia, Perpetua and many others followed. Tertullian wrote, ‘The blood of the martyrs is the seed of the Church.’ The more the Church was persecuted, the more she grew, as people were impressed by the martyrs’ courage and joy.”", "Say: “Not everyone was strong. Some, under torture, gave in, offering sacrifice or handing over the Scriptures. They were called the lapsed. After persecution ended, the Church debated how to treat them, and decided that they could return after penance. This is the origin of the sacrament of Reconciliation’s public penance. Christians also met in the catacombs, underground burial places in Rome, where they prayed, celebrated Mass over the tombs of the martyrs, and used symbols like the fish (ichthys) and the Chi-Rho. Today, there are still martyrs; in the 20th century there were more Christian martyrs than in all earlier centuries.”"],
    ask=["What does ‘martyr’ mean? (Witness.)", "Why were Christians persecuted? (They refused to worship the emperor and the gods.)", "What did Tertullian say about martyrs? (Their blood is the seed of the Church.)"],
    practice=["Read selections from Perpetua’s diary or the Martyrdom of Polycarp (a short excerpt).", "In pairs, answer: What was she asked to do? What did she refuse? Why?", "Write a short statement of what you would stand up for.", "Share one."],
    close="Pray: “Holy martyrs, pray for us.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for Christians who are persecuted today.”")),

'8-2-2': dict(
  saint="Nicholas of Myra: a bishop who, tradition says, was at Nicaea and who gave in secret.",
  tryit=["Order the events from Milan to Nicaea.", "Say what each Council added to the Creed.", "Write the Creed line it defended."],
  saintStory="Nicholas lived in the fourth century in Myra, in what is now Turkey. He was born into a wealthy family and orphaned young. According to old accounts, he used his inheritance to help the poor. In one story, he learned of a man who was too poor to give his three daughters a dowry, and who was about to sell them into slavery. In the night, Nicholas threw a bag of gold through the window, three times.\nHe became bishop of Myra, and was imprisoned during the persecution under Diocletian. Tradition says he attended the Council of Nicaea in 325, where he defended the faith against Arius. The same stories say that, passionately, he struck Arius. The more reliable fact is that Nicholas was known for charity and for protecting the poor and the falsely accused.\nHe died around 343. Stories of his gift-giving spread, and he became Santa Claus in many countries. He is patron of children and sailors.",
  script=dict(
    open="Display a coin or medal with Constantine’s image. Say, “The first Christian emperor. What would you ask him if he were alive?” Collect questions and see which ones the lesson answers.",
    teach=["Say: “In 312, before a battle at the Milvian Bridge, Constantine saw a sign, the Chi-Rho, and the words ‘In this sign conquer.’ He won, and in 313, with Licinius, he issued the Edict of Milan, which gave Christians freedom of worship and returned their property. He later supported the Church with buildings, such as the Lateran basilica, and moved the capital to Constantinople. In 380, under Theodosius, Christianity became the official religion. Constantine was baptized on his deathbed in 337.”", "Say: “With freedom came new challenges: how to be faithful when it was safe, and how to settle disputes about doctrine. Arius, a priest in Alexandria, taught that the Son was created, ‘there was a time when he was not.’ This would make Jesus less than God. To settle it, Constantine called the first ecumenical council at Nicaea in 325, with about 300 bishops. They declared that the Son is ‘begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father.’ They wrote the first part of the Nicene Creed. Athanasius defended it for decades.”", "Say: “Other councils followed. Constantinople I (381) defined the divinity of the Holy Spirit and completed the Creed. Ephesus (431) declared Mary Theotokos, Mother of God. Chalcedon (451) taught that Christ has two natures in one Person. These councils protected the faith about the Trinity and the Incarnation. The councils show how the Church works: bishops gather, with the Pope or his legates, to define the faith. The Holy Spirit guides the Church to preserve truth. We still pray their words in the Creed every Sunday.”"],
    ask=["What did the Edict of Milan do? (Gave Christians freedom of worship.)", "What did Arius teach? (That the Son was a created being.)", "What did Nicaea declare? (The Son is consubstantial with the Father.)"],
    practice=["Make a timeline with the four councils: Nicaea 325, Constantinople 381, Ephesus 431, Chalcedon 451.", "Beside each, write the heresy and the Church’s answer.", "Then find the matching line in the Nicene Creed.", "Pray that line aloud."],
    close="Pray the Nicene Creed together. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up the Nicene Creed and underline lines from Nicaea.”")),

'8-2-3': dict(
  saint="John Chrysostom: the golden-mouthed preacher who told the emperor’s court the truth.",
  tryit=["Match each Father to his work.", "Quote one line.", "Say why the Fathers matter."],
  saintStory="John was born in Antioch around 349. His father died when he was young, and his mother Anthusa raised him. He studied rhetoric and could have had a career in law, but became a monk and hermit, living in a cave for two years until his health failed. He was ordained a priest, and for twelve years preached in Antioch, going through the Bible verse by verse. Because of his eloquence, he was called Chrysostom, “golden mouth.”\nIn 397 he was made archbishop of Constantinople, against his will. He reformed the clergy, sold the palace’s luxury items and gave the money to hospitals. He denounced the pride and cruelty of the rich, and criticized the empress Eudoxia. She had him exiled. He was brought back after an earthquake, then exiled again.\nOn a forced march in 407, in bad weather, he collapsed. His last words were: “Glory to God for all things.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “If you could read the letters of someone who knew Jesus’ friends, would you? The Church Fathers are those writers.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Fathers of the Church are early Christian writers and bishops, from about AD 100 to 700, whose teaching is trusted as faithful. The Church honors them because they were close to the apostles’ time, they defended and explained the faith, and they were holy. They include the Apostolic Fathers, who knew the apostles’ disciples: Clement of Rome, Ignatius of Antioch, Polycarp. The Apologists, who defended the faith: Justin Martyr. And the great Fathers: in the East, Athanasius, Basil, Gregory of Nazianzus, Gregory of Nyssa and John Chrysostom; in the West, Ambrose, Jerome, Augustine and Gregory the Great.”", "Say: “Each gave a gift. Athanasius defended the divinity of Christ. Basil wrote about the Holy Spirit and started monasteries. Chrysostom preached on Scripture. Ambrose taught the Mass and hymns. Jerome translated the Bible. Augustine, in his Confessions and City of God, explored grace, sin, and God’s love: ‘You have made us for yourself, and our hearts are restless until they rest in you.’ Gregory the Great organized the Church’s care for the poor and sent missionaries to England. The Church calls the greatest teachers Doctors of the Church.”", "Say: “Why do the Fathers matter? When they agree, it is strong evidence of what the apostles taught. They show that the Catholic faith was not invented later. For example, on the Eucharist, Ignatius wrote that it is the flesh of Christ, and Justin that it is not common bread. On the Pope’s role, Irenaeus pointed to Rome. On Mary, Irenaeus called her the new Eve. They are also good reading: Augustine’s Confessions is still a best-seller. Their words fill the Liturgy of the Hours’ second reading.”"],
    ask=["Who are the Fathers of the Church? (Early Christian writers whose teaching is trusted.)", "Name two Fathers and what they did. (Athanasius defended Christ’s divinity; Jerome translated the Bible.)", "Why do they matter? (They show what the apostles taught.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards with Father names and short quotes.", "Teams match each Father to his contribution.", "Pick one quote that speaks to you and copy it onto a bookmark.", "Explain why you chose it."],
    close="Pray the prayer of Augustine: “You have made us for yourself, O Lord, and our hearts are restless until they rest in you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Read a line from a Church Father in the Office of Readings.”")),

'8-3-1': dict(
  saint="Benedict: the father of Western monks, who taught “pray and work.”",
  tryit=["Plan a day in a Benedictine monastery.", "Mark prayer, work, study and rest.", "Say what Benedict’s Rule gives."],
  saintStory="Benedict was born around 480 in Nursia, Italy. As a student in Rome, he was shocked by the wild life of the other students and left to live as a hermit in a cave at Subiaco, where he stayed for three years. Others heard of his holiness and asked him to lead their monastery. Some monks, not liking his strictness, tried to poison him; the cup shattered when he made the sign of the cross over it.\nHe left and founded twelve small monasteries, then moved to Monte Cassino, where he wrote the Rule of Saint Benedict. It is a short, practical guide: the monks pray the Divine Office, work with their hands, read Scripture, eat together, and obey an abbot. It begins, “Listen carefully, my son, to the master’s instructions, and attend to them with the ear of your heart.”\nBenedict died around 547. His Rule became the pattern for monasteries across Europe. His motto: Ora et labora, “pray and work.” He is patron of Europe.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ring a small bell at the start. Say, “A monk hears this bell eight times a day and stops what he is doing. What do you stop for?” Take answers and write them down.",
    teach=["Say: “After Rome fell, Europe entered what we call the Middle Ages, about 500 to 1500. Monasteries became centers of faith and civilization. Monks copied manuscripts by hand, preserving the Bible and works of ancient writers. They farmed and drained marshes. They ran schools, hospitals and guesthouses. St. Benedict’s Rule gave them a balanced life: the day is divided into prayer (the Liturgy of the Hours), work and study (lectio divina). The monks took vows of stability, fidelity to monastic life, and obedience.”", "Say: “Missionary monks spread the faith. Patrick, who as a young man was kidnapped and enslaved in Ireland, escaped, and later returned as bishop to convert the Irish. Irish monks such as Columba and Columbanus founded monasteries in Scotland and on the Continent. Pope Gregory the Great sent Augustine of Canterbury to England in 597. Boniface, an English monk, went to Germany and cut down the sacred oak at Geismar; he was martyred in 754. Cyril and Methodius evangelized the Slavs. These missionaries brought the Gospel to Europe and shaped the culture.”", "Say: “In the 800s, Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne as emperor on Christmas Day 800. Charlemagne supported schools and the Church. Later, new religious orders arose to renew the Church: the Cistercians, led by Bernard of Clairvaux, the Franciscans and Dominicans in the 1200s. The Middle Ages were not ‘dark’ to those who lived in them: they built cathedrals, founded universities and developed Gregorian chant. But they were not perfect: there was also corruption and war. The Church kept calling for reform.”"],
    ask=["What did Benedict’s Rule teach? (Pray and work, in community under an abbot.)", "What did monasteries preserve? (Books, learning, farming, hospitality.)", "Who evangelized Ireland? (Patrick.)"],
    practice=["Draw a clock of a monastery day: rising for Vigils, Lauds, work, Terce, Sext, None, work or study, Vespers, supper, Compline, sleep.", "Mark prayer in one color, work in another, study in a third.", "Write which part you would find hardest and which easiest.", "Compare with your own day."],
    close="Pray Compline’s opening: “O God, come to my assistance. Lord, make haste to help me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray and work: give your homework to God.”")),

'8-3-2': dict(
  saint="Thomas Aquinas: a Dominican friar who joined faith and reason in the Summa.",
  tryit=["Read an article of the Summa in four steps.", "Write your own objection and reply.", "Say how faith and reason cooperate."],
  saintStory="Thomas was born around 1225 in a castle in Italy, the son of a count. When he was about nineteen, he decided to join the Dominicans, a new order of preachers. His family was furious, since a noble son was supposed to become a Benedictine abbot. His brothers captured him and held him for over a year in the family castle, even sending a woman to tempt him. He drove her away with a burning stick. Eventually he escaped, and went to study with Albert the Great.\nIn class, his silence earned him the nickname “the dumb ox.” Albert said, “This ox will one day bellow so loud that it will fill the whole world.” Thomas taught in Paris and Italy, and wrote the Summa Theologiae, a vast, orderly summary of the faith. He used reason and the philosopher Aristotle to explain the faith.\nIn December 1273, after Mass, he stopped writing. “All that I have written seems like straw compared to what I have seen.” He died in 1274 on his way to a council. He wrote the hymns Pange Lingua and Tantum Ergo.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a photo of Chartres Cathedral’s rose window. Say, “Someone spent a lifetime building this and never saw it finished. Why do people work on things that long?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The high Middle Ages, about 1100 to 1300, were a time of great growth. Cathedrals rose, such as Chartres, Notre Dame and Cologne, with stained glass that taught the Bible to people who could not read. Pilgrims walked to Rome, Compostela and Jerusalem. Guilds of workers formed. And universities were founded: Bologna, Paris, Oxford and Cambridge. They were started by the Church, to study theology, law, medicine and the arts. The students were often clerics, and the professors included saints.”", "Say: “New religious orders brought renewal. Francis of Assisi, who gave up wealth in 1206, started the Franciscans, who lived in poverty and preached in the streets. Dominic Guzmán founded the Dominicans to preach and teach. Clare of Assisi started the Poor Clares. These friars lived among the people in cities, not in remote monasteries. The Church also had saints like Elizabeth of Hungary, Louis IX of France, and Catherine of Siena.”", "Say: “Thomas Aquinas taught that faith and reason do not conflict, since God is the author of both. Reason can show that God exists, and faith goes further, revealing truths like the Trinity. ‘Grace does not destroy nature but perfects it.’ In the Summa, he asks questions like ‘Does God exist?’ and follows a pattern: the question, the objections, the answer from authority (‘On the contrary…’), his own reply, and answers to each objection. He gave five ways to show that God exists, from motion, causation, contingency, degrees of perfection and design. The Church calls Thomas ‘Doctor of the Church’ and the Angelic Doctor.”"],
    ask=["What did the Church found in the Middle Ages? (Universities, hospitals, schools.)", "What did Aquinas teach about faith and reason? (They cannot conflict, since God is the author of both.)", "What were the new orders? (Franciscans and Dominicans.)"],
    practice=["Choose a question, like “Is it right to be angry?” or “Do we need God for morality?”.", "Write the question at the top, then an objection, then “On the contrary…” and your reply.", "Answer the objection.", "Share with a partner, who argues the objection."],
    close="Pray the Tantum Ergo or Pange Lingua verse. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Explain to someone why faith and reason go together.”")),

'8-3-3': dict(
  saint="Cyril and Methodius: two brothers who gave the Slavs an alphabet and the liturgy in their own language.",
  tryit=["Map the East and the West.", "List what the two churches share.", "List what divided them."],
  saintStory="Cyril and Methodius were brothers born in Thessalonica, a Greek city, in the ninth century. Cyril, whose baptismal name was Constantine, was a scholar. Methodius was an administrator. In 863 the prince of Moravia, in Central Europe, asked the Byzantine emperor for missionaries who could teach in the Slavic language.\nThe brothers created an alphabet for the Slavs, called Glagolitic, and translated the Gospels and liturgy into Old Church Slavonic. Some Latin-speaking clergy opposed them, arguing that liturgy should only be in Hebrew, Greek or Latin. The brothers traveled to Rome, and Pope Adrian II approved their work. Cyril died in Rome in 869. Methodius was made archbishop and continued to work for sixteen more years.\nThey are patrons of Europe, with Benedict, and honored by both Catholics and Orthodox. Their story belongs to the time when East and West were still together.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a map of Europe and the Mediterranean. Ask, “Where was Rome? Where was Constantinople? Why might two such different cities, with different languages, have trouble staying together?”",
    teach=["Say: “From the beginning, the Church had five great centers called patriarchates: Rome, Constantinople, Alexandria, Antioch and Jerusalem. In the East they spoke Greek, in the West Latin. Over centuries, they drifted apart in language, culture and politics. After Rome fell in the West, the Empire continued in the East, with its capital at Constantinople. There were disputes about the authority of the Pope, and about the words ‘and the Son’ (the filioque) added to the Creed in the West, stating that the Spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son.”", "Say: “In 1054, the Pope’s legate, Cardinal Humbert, and the patriarch of Constantinople, Michael Cerularius, excommunicated each other. This is called the East-West Schism. It was not a single event, but the end of a long period of division. In 1204, during the Fourth Crusade, Western crusaders sacked Constantinople, which deepened the wound. Attempts at reunion, such as the Council of Florence in 1439, did not hold. Today the Orthodox Churches (Greek, Russian, Serbian, Romanian and others) are separated from Rome.”", "Say: “The Orthodox and Catholic Churches share much: the Creed, seven sacraments, bishops with apostolic succession, devotion to Mary and the saints, and the Real Presence. The main differences are the authority of the Pope and the filioque. Eastern Catholic Churches, such as the Maronite, Melkite and Ukrainian Greek Catholic, are in communion with Rome while keeping their own rites and traditions. Pope John Paul II said that the Church must ‘breathe with her two lungs,’ East and West. Popes and patriarchs have met in recent decades, and Catholics pray for unity.”"],
    ask=["When was the East-West Schism? (1054, ending a long drift.)", "What do Orthodox and Catholics share? (Creed, sacraments, bishops, Mary and the saints.)", "What are Eastern Catholic Churches? (Churches in communion with Rome with their own rites.)"],
    practice=["Draw a Venn diagram: Catholic, Orthodox, and what they share.", "Fill in the overlap with at least six things we share, and the sides with differences.", "Add one thing you admire about Eastern worship, such as icons, incense, or chanting.", "Write a prayer for unity."],
    close="Pray: “That they may all be one.” (John 17:21.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for Orthodox Christians.”")),

'8-4-1': dict(
  saint="Peter Canisius: a Jesuit who taught the faith by catechism and kindness in a divided Germany.",
  tryit=["List the causes of the Reformation.", "Mark what was real reform and what departed from the faith.", "Write what Catholics and Protestants share."],
  saintStory="Peter Canisius was born in 1521 in the Netherlands, the year Martin Luther was condemned at the Diet of Worms. He studied law, and then joined the Jesuits, the first Dutchman to do so. He was sent to Germany, where many were leaving the Catholic Church, and spent his life there.\nHe was a professor, preacher and adviser. He wrote three catechisms, clear and simple, that were used for centuries; by one count he had more than 200 editions. He founded schools. He believed the best way to respond was with charity and clear teaching, not insults. He insisted that Catholics should be fair toward Protestants, and he asked that sermons not be used to attack.\nHe worked at the Council of Trent and helped keep Southern Germany Catholic. He died in 1597 and is a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “What do you do when you love something that has serious problems, like a school or team you belong to? Leave, or work for change? Why?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “By the early 1500s, the Church needed reform. Some bishops were worldly and some clergy were poorly trained. Church offices were bought and sold (simony). Many people were not well instructed in the faith. In 1517, Pope Leo X was raising money to rebuild St. Peter’s Basilica, and preachers like Johann Tetzel were selling indulgences in a way that suggested forgiveness could be purchased. An Augustinian friar and professor named Martin Luther posted 95 theses at Wittenberg, questioning this practice. He wanted a debate.”", "Say: “It escalated. Luther also taught that we are justified by faith alone (sola fide), that Scripture alone is the authority (sola scriptura), and rejected the Pope’s authority and most sacraments. He was excommunicated in 1521. In Switzerland, Zwingli and John Calvin led other movements. In England, Henry VIII broke with Rome in 1534 to secure a divorce, making himself head of the Church; Thomas More and John Fisher died rather than accept it. The movement spread, and Europe was torn by wars of religion. Today there are about 900 million Protestants.”", "Say: “The Church’s view is that the Reformers’ complaints about abuses were often right, but their remedy broke the unity of the Church and departed from some teachings. Catholics teach that we are justified by grace through faith, but faith must be alive in love and good works (James 2:24). Scripture and Tradition together, interpreted by the Magisterium, are the rule of faith. Today Catholics and Protestants have much in common: faith in Christ, the Trinity, Scripture and Baptism. In 1999, Catholics and Lutherans issued a Joint Declaration on Justification. We are called to respect and to pray for unity, and to avoid caricatures.”"],
    ask=["What sparked the Reformation in 1517? (The sale of indulgences and Luther’s 95 theses.)", "What are two Reformation slogans? (Sola fide, sola scriptura.)", "What do Catholics and Protestants share? (Faith in Christ, the Trinity, Scripture, Baptism.)"],
    practice=["Make a table with three columns: causes, responses, results.", "Fill in with at least four items each.", "Mark in green what is genuine reform and in red what departed from Catholic teaching.", "Write a sentence on how to talk respectfully with a Protestant friend."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, heal the wounds of division.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a Christian from another tradition what they love about their church.”")),

'8-4-2': dict(
  saint="Ignatius of Loyola: a wounded soldier who became a master of discernment and founded the Jesuits.",
  tryit=["List the reforms of Trent.", "Match each to a problem.", "Try the Examen."],
  saintStory="Ignatius was born in 1491 in the Basque region of Spain, a young nobleman and soldier, proud and vain. In 1521, at the battle of Pamplona, a cannonball shattered his leg. During a long recovery at the castle of Loyola, he asked for books of romance, but the only ones available were a life of Christ and lives of the saints. He noticed that when he thought of worldly deeds, he was excited and then empty; when he thought of following Christ like the saints, he felt lasting peace. This was the start of his method of discernment.\nHe gave up his sword, spent time at Manresa in prayer, wrote the Spiritual Exercises, and studied in Paris. There he gathered a small group of friends, including Francis Xavier, and in 1540 the Pope approved their order, the Society of Jesus. The Jesuits took a special vow of obedience to the Pope and ran schools around the world.\nIgnatius died in 1556. His motto: Ad majorem Dei gloriam, “for the greater glory of God.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Read aloud the opening line of the decree on the Eucharist from Trent, or a short paragraph from the Roman Catechism. Say, “Hear the tone. These are bishops trying to put a house in order.” Ask what they were afraid of losing.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church answered the Reformation with the Council of Trent, held in Trent, Italy, in three periods from 1545 to 1563 under Popes Paul III, Julius III and Pius IV. It is sometimes called the Counter-Reformation or Catholic Reformation. The Council did two things: it clarified Catholic doctrine, and it reformed abuses.”", "Say: “Doctrine: Scripture and Tradition are both sources of revelation; the Church interprets. Justification comes by grace, and good works are fruit and merit. There are seven sacraments. The Mass is a true sacrifice, and Christ is truly present in the Eucharist (transubstantiation). The Church confirmed the canon of 73 books, and affirmed the Latin Vulgate. Reform: bishops must live in their dioceses and preach. Seminaries must be set up in every diocese to train priests; before this, many priests had no formal training. Selling church offices and indulgences was condemned. The Council issued the Roman Catechism, a standard Missal (1570), and the Breviary.”", "Say: “New and renewed orders drove the reform. The Jesuits, founded by Ignatius of Loyola, were educators and missionaries. The Capuchins renewed the Franciscans. Teresa of Avila and John of the Cross reformed the Carmelites. Philip Neri founded the Oratory. Charles Borromeo, Francis de Sales and Vincent de Paul led by example. The Church also saw the great art of the Baroque era, which proclaimed the faith in beauty. The reform was a renewal from within, which lasted over three centuries until Vatican II.”"],
    ask=["What did Trent do? (Clarified doctrine and reformed abuses.)", "What were seminaries for? (Training priests.)", "Who founded the Jesuits? (Ignatius of Loyola.)"],
    practice=["Prepare a chart: Problem, Trent’s response.", "Fill in: uneducated priests / seminaries; confusion about doctrine / Roman Catechism; absent bishops / residence; abuses of indulgences / reform.", "Try Ignatius’s Examen for five minutes: thank God for the day, review your day, notice what was life-giving and what was not, ask forgiveness, resolve for tomorrow.", "Share one insight."],
    close="Pray the Suscipe of Ignatius: “Take, Lord, receive all my liberty.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do the Examen each night.”")),

'8-4-3': dict(
  saint="Francis Xavier: a Jesuit who carried the Gospel to India and Japan.",
  tryit=["Map the missions.", "Mark each missionary and place.", "Say what they brought and how they adapted."],
  saintStory="Francis Xavier was born in 1506 in Navarre, Spain. He was a bright, ambitious student at the University of Paris, hoping for fame. His roommate Ignatius of Loyola kept asking him, “What does it profit a man if he gains the whole world and loses his soul?” In time, Francis was won over. In 1534 he and Ignatius and five others took vows at Montmartre.\nIn 1541 he sailed for India, a thirteen-month voyage. He worked in Goa, then among the poor fishermen on the southern coast, where he taught the Our Father and Creed by song and had children go door to door ringing a bell. He then went on to Malacca, the Moluccas, and in 1549 to Japan, where he learned that the local culture respected scholars and rank. He planned to enter China but died in 1552 on an island off its coast, at forty-six.\nHe is patron of missions, and is said to have baptized tens of thousands.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a world map. Ask, “In 1492, Columbus sailed. How did the faith get to the Americas, Asia and Africa? Who went?” Take guesses.",
    teach=["Say: “The age of exploration, from 1492, opened new worlds. Spain and Portugal sent explorers, and the Church sent missionaries. Franciscans, Dominicans, Augustinians and Jesuits traveled to Mexico, Peru, Brazil, India, China, Japan, the Philippines and Africa. In 1531, Mary appeared at Guadalupe to Juan Diego, a native man, and millions of Native Americans converted. Missionaries learned local languages and wrote dictionaries and grammars. They established schools and hospitals.”", "Say: “There were also failures. Some colonizers abused and enslaved native peoples, and some missionaries went along with this. But others defended the natives. Bartolomé de las Casas, a Dominican, denounced the cruelty. Pope Paul III in 1537 declared that the Indians are truly men and must not be enslaved. Peter Claver served enslaved Africans. Jesuits in Paraguay built ‘reductions,’ self-governing communities that protected native peoples. Matteo Ricci in China adapted, wearing Chinese scholars’ robes and learning the language and philosophy. Not all of this mission history is glorious. The Church acknowledges the sins of her members.”", "Say: “Missionary saints include Francis Xavier, Rose of Lima (the first saint of the Americas), Martin de Porres, Junípero Serra, Isaac Jogues and the Canadian martyrs, Kateri Tekakwitha, the Japanese martyrs of Nagasaki (1597, 26 martyrs; and later hidden Christians for 250 years), Paul Miki, and the Korean and Vietnamese martyrs. The missions made the Church truly worldwide. Jesus told his disciples to go to all nations. Every Catholic shares in this mission by prayer, giving and witness.”"],
    ask=["Who defended the rights of native peoples? (Las Casas, Pope Paul III, Peter Claver.)", "Who was the first saint of the Americas? (Rose of Lima.)", "What did Matteo Ricci do in China? (Adapted to Chinese culture.)"],
    practice=["Give each pair a blank world map.", "Mark: Goa and Japan (Xavier), Mexico (Guadalupe), Peru (Rose of Lima), China (Ricci), Paraguay (reductions), Canada (martyrs).", "Beside each, write the missionary and one fact.", "Write a sentence on what you admire about missionaries and what troubles you."],
    close="Pray the Memorare for missionaries or “Lord, send workers into your harvest.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for missionaries and give to the missions.”")),

'8-5-1': dict(
  saint="John XXIII: the “good pope” who opened the windows of the Church.",
  tryit=["List the four constitutions of Vatican II.", "Match each to a change.", "Find one at your parish."],
  saintStory="Angelo Roncalli was born in 1881 to a poor farming family in northern Italy. He became a priest, served as a stretcher-bearer in World War I, then as a Vatican diplomat in Bulgaria, Turkey and Greece. During World War II he helped save Jewish refugees. He became cardinal of Venice, and in 1958, at seventy-six, was elected pope, expected to be a short “caretaker.”\nThree months later, he announced a council. Many were surprised; no council had been held since 1870. He said he wanted to “open the windows of the Church to let in some fresh air.” Vatican II opened in October 1962. He was warm, jovial and humble, and was loved by people of all faiths.\nJohn XXIII died of cancer in 1963, after the first session. He was named a saint in 2014, together with John Paul II. His last words in a letter: “Be good, be gentle, be at peace.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Open a window in the classroom, or show a picture of one. Ask, “What happens when a room is stuffy and we open a window? John XXIII used this picture for the Church.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Second Vatican Council met in Rome from 1962 to 1965 under John XXIII and then Paul VI. About 2,500 bishops from around the world attended. Pope John said its aim was aggiornamento, ‘bringing up to date,’ and not changing the faith, but presenting it in a way the modern world can hear. The council produced 16 documents. It did not change doctrine, but renewed the Church’s life.”", "Say: “The four major documents are called constitutions. Lumen Gentium, on the Church, describes her as the People of God, with a universal call to holiness, and the role of the laity. Dei Verbum, on Revelation, explains Scripture and Tradition, and encourages all to read the Bible. Sacrosanctum Concilium, on the liturgy, led to the Mass in local languages, greater participation, and a richer use of Scripture. Gaudium et Spes, on the Church in the modern world, says ‘the joys and hopes, the griefs and anxieties of the people of this age… are the joys and hopes, the griefs and anxieties of the followers of Christ.’”", "Say: “Other documents deal with ecumenism (Unitatis Redintegratio), non-Christian religions (Nostra Aetate, which condemned antisemitism and affirmed what is true in other religions), religious freedom (Dignitatis Humanae), and the role of laity (Apostolicam Actuositatem). Many Catholics today can still be confused about what Vatican II taught. Pope Benedict XVI said we should read it with a ‘hermeneutic of reform’ in continuity with the whole tradition. The council was not a break with the past but a renewal. Its main points: holiness for all, active participation in the liturgy, reading Scripture, dialogue with the world, and mission.”"],
    ask=["Who called Vatican II? (John XXIII.)", "What are the four constitutions? (Lumen Gentium, Dei Verbum, Sacrosanctum Concilium, Gaudium et Spes.)", "What does Gaudium et Spes say? (The joys and hopes of people are those of Christians too.)"],
    practice=["Make a four-row chart: document, subject, one change.", "Fill from the lesson and the Catechism.", "Look for one visible sign of Vatican II in your parish: Mass in English, lay readers, the Bible readings.", "Write one thing the Council asks of you."],
    close="Pray: “Come, Holy Spirit, renew your Church.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask an older person what changed after Vatican II.”")),

'8-5-2': dict(
  saint="Katharine Drexel: an heiress who gave away a fortune to educate Native and Black Americans.",
  tryit=["Match each principle of social teaching to an example.", "Apply two to a real issue.", "Plan one action."],
  saintStory="Katharine Drexel was born in 1858 in Philadelphia, to one of America’s richest banking families. Her parents were generous Catholics who opened their home to the poor twice a week. As a young woman, Katharine toured Europe, and then visited the Western reservations where she saw the poverty of Native Americans. She asked Pope Leo XIII to send missionaries; he replied, “Why not become a missionary yourself?”\nAt thirty-three, she took vows, and founded the Sisters of the Blessed Sacrament for Indians and Colored People. She used her inheritance to build schools and missions across the country, including Xavier University in New Orleans, the first Black Catholic university in the United States. She faced opposition and threats, including from groups who tried to burn down a school.\nShe gave away about twenty million dollars in her lifetime. She died in 1955, at ninety-six, and was named a saint in 2000.",
  script=dict(
    open="Display a news photo of a food bank line and one of a corporate headquarters. Say, “Both are true about our economy. Who should speak for the people in the first picture?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “In 1891, Pope Leo XIII wrote Rerum Novarum, ‘On the New Things,’ the first great social encyclical. The Industrial Revolution had brought wealth for some and misery for many workers in factories. Leo said that workers have rights: to a just wage, safe conditions and to form unions. Property is a right, but it has a social purpose. Both socialism and unchecked capitalism are wrong. Popes since then have updated the teaching: Pius XI, John XXIII, Paul VI, John Paul II (Centesimus Annus), Benedict XVI, and Francis (Laudato Si’).”", "Say: “The Church has seven themes of Catholic social teaching. One: the life and dignity of the human person. Two: call to family, community and participation. Three: rights and responsibilities. Four: the option for the poor and vulnerable, putting the needs of the poor first. Five: the dignity of work and the rights of workers. Six: solidarity, we are one human family. Seven: care for God’s creation. These come from Scripture and reason. They are tools for judging social problems.”", "Say: “The principles also include the common good, the conditions that let everyone flourish, and subsidiarity, which means decisions should be made at the lowest level able to handle them, so that families and communities are not replaced by the state. Pope Francis in Laudato Si’ calls us to hear ‘both the cry of the earth and the cry of the poor.’ Social teaching is not a political party, but it does challenge every party. It asks us to see Christ in the poor, to work for justice, and to live simply.”"],
    ask=["What was Rerum Novarum about? (The rights of workers.)", "What is the option for the poor? (Giving priority to the needs of the poor.)", "What is subsidiarity? (Decisions should be made at the lowest competent level.)"],
    practice=["Write the seven themes on cards, and the examples on others: food bank, minimum wage, recycling, pro-life work, refugee welcome, volunteer in the community, voting.", "Teams match them.", "Choose a real issue in your community and apply two principles to it.", "Plan one action."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make us instruments of your justice and peace.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Support a cause that helps the poor.”")),

'8-5-3': dict(
  saint="Josaphat Kuntsevych: an Eastern-rite archbishop who died working for unity.",
  tryit=["Match each continent to its Catholics.", "Name a saint from each.", "Say what the Church’s worldwide shape means."],
  saintStory="Josaphat Kuntsevych was born around 1580 in what is now Ukraine, then part of Poland-Lithuania. As a young man he was a merchant’s apprentice, and then became a Basilian monk in the Eastern tradition. In 1596, some years before, the Union of Brest had united many Orthodox bishops in the region with Rome, creating the Ukrainian Greek Catholic Church, which kept its Eastern liturgy. But the union was controversial; many Orthodox opposed it.\nJosaphat became archbishop of Polotsk and worked to bring unity. He preached, reformed the clergy, and wrote a catechism. His work made him popular with many, but angered opponents. In 1623, a mob in Vitebsk attacked his residence and killed him with an axe.\nHe was the first Eastern Catholic to be formally canonized by Rome, in 1867. His story reminds us of the beauty and the pain of the divisions among Christians.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How many Catholics are in the world? Where do they live? Guess.” Take guesses. “About 1.4 billion. Where do most live?”",
    teach=["Say: “Today the Catholic Church has about 1.4 billion members, about one in six people. More than 60 percent are in the Americas and Africa. Latin America has the most Catholics, with Brazil and Mexico leading. Africa is growing fastest. In Asia, the Philippines is largely Catholic, and there are minorities in India, Vietnam, Korea and China. In Europe, the numbers have fallen. The center of the Church’s population has moved from Europe to the Global South.”", "Say: “The Church is one, but with many rites. The Latin (Roman) Rite is the largest. The Eastern Catholic Churches are 23 Churches in communion with the Pope, with their own liturgy, law and traditions: Ukrainian, Maronite, Melkite, Syro-Malabar, Syro-Malankara, Chaldean, Coptic, Ethiopian and others. They have their own patriarchs and bishops, and some have married priests. We should respect and learn about these Churches, since they bring the Church’s ancient traditions.”", "Say: “In many places, the Church is under pressure. In China, the Church is divided between government-registered and underground communities. In the Middle East, Christians have fled war and persecution; in Nigeria, Pakistan and elsewhere, Christians face violence. In Europe and North America, many have left the practice of the faith. The Church is also growing in places like Africa and parts of Asia. The Pope serves as the visible sign of unity. Through Catholic Relief Services, missionary societies and the Propagation of the Faith, we support the Church worldwide. We’re one family.”"],
    ask=["Where are most Catholics today? (The Americas and Africa.)", "What are Eastern Catholic Churches? (Churches in communion with Rome with their own rites.)", "How can we support the worldwide Church? (Pray, give, learn, and serve.)"],
    practice=["Divide a world map into continents.", "For each, write an approximate Catholic number, a country, and a saint or event.", "Choose one place where Christians suffer, and write a prayer for them.", "Look up one Eastern Catholic Church and write three facts."],
    close="Pray for the Church throughout the world and for persecuted Christians. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Learn about an Eastern Catholic Church.”")),

'8-6-1': dict(
  saint="Basil the Great: the bishop who wrote the classic book on the Holy Spirit.",
  tryit=["Fill in the Holy Spirit’s names and symbols.", "Link each to a Scripture passage.", "Say what the Spirit does in the Church."],
  saintStory="Basil was born around 330 in Caesarea, Cappadocia (now Turkey), to a family of saints: his grandmother Macrina, his parents, and his brothers Gregory of Nyssa and Peter of Sebaste, and sister Macrina were all honored as saints. He studied in Athens, where he met Gregory of Nazianzus. Giving up a career in rhetoric, he traveled to visit monks in Egypt and Syria, and then started a community on his family’s estate.\nIn 370 he became bishop of Caesarea. He fought against Arianism, which denied the divinity of Christ, and against emperors who favored it. He set up a huge complex called the Basiliad that included a hospital, a hostel for travelers and a workshop for the poor. When a famine struck, he preached and organized relief.\nHis book On the Holy Spirit defended the Spirit’s full divinity: the Spirit is to be worshiped and glorified with the Father and the Son. He died in 379. His liturgy is still used in Eastern churches.",
  script=dict(
    open="Wave a sheet of paper or blow gently across a candle flame. Say, “The Spirit is described as wind and as fire. What can you see of wind or fire? What do you see only by what it does?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Holy Spirit is the third Person of the Trinity: God, equal to the Father and the Son. The Creed says, ‘I believe in the Holy Spirit, the Lord, the giver of life, who proceeds from the Father and the Son, who with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified, who has spoken through the prophets.’ He is not an impersonal force. He is a Person, with whom we can have a relationship. Jesus called him the Advocate, Paraclete (John 14:16), the Spirit of truth.”", "Say: “Scripture uses symbols of the Spirit. Water: he gives life and cleanses (John 7:37-39). Anointing with oil: he strengthens and consecrates. Fire: he purifies and sets hearts aflame (Acts 2). Cloud and light: his presence and power, as at Jesus’ Transfiguration. The seal: he marks us permanently in Baptism, Confirmation and Orders. The hand: laying on of hands. The dove: peace and new creation, at Jesus’ baptism. Wind or breath: he gives life, as when God breathed on Adam and Jesus breathed on the apostles (John 20:22).”", "Say: “What does the Spirit do? He inspired the Scriptures, speaking through the prophets. He overshadowed Mary. He descended on Jesus at his baptism. At Pentecost, he came upon the Church and still guides her: he leads the Church into all truth (John 16:13), he gives life in the sacraments, he forms the saints, he gives gifts and charisms to the faithful for service. He dwells in us as in a temple (1 Corinthians 6:19). He prays in us when we do not know how (Romans 8:26). The Church is the work of the Spirit. We invoke him often: ‘Come, Holy Spirit.’”"],
    ask=["Who is the Holy Spirit? (The third Person of the Trinity, God.)", "Name three symbols of the Spirit. (Water, fire, dove, oil, wind.)", "What does the Spirit do in the Church? (Guides, gives life in the sacraments, forms saints.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: Symbol, Scripture, What it shows.", "Fill in water, oil, fire, dove, wind, seal, hand.", "Draw each one.", "Write a prayer to the Holy Spirit using one of the symbols."],
    close="Pray the sequence “Come, Holy Spirit” or the Veni Creator. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray to the Holy Spirit before homework.”")),

'8-6-2': dict(
  saint="Joan of Arc: a farm girl who showed fortitude, counsel and piety at nineteen.",
  tryit=["Match each gift to a moment in Joan’s life.", "Write one example for yourself.", "Name the fruit that follows."],
  saintStory="Joan was born around 1412 in Domrémy, in northeastern France, to a peasant family, during the Hundred Years’ War, when England controlled much of France. She was a devout girl who heard voices, which she said were St. Michael, St. Catherine and St. Margaret, telling her to help the Dauphin Charles to be crowned king.\nAt sixteen, she went to the local commander, who scoffed at her. But she persisted, and was taken to Charles, and examined by theologians. She was given armor and a banner, and at seventeen led troops to lift the siege of Orléans in nine days. She then accompanied Charles to Reims, where he was crowned in 1429.\nIn 1430, she was captured by Burgundians and sold to the English. At a trial for heresy at Rouen, she answered clearly and bravely, despite being an unlettered girl. She was burned at the stake on May 30, 1431, aged about nineteen, calling out “Jesus.” In 1456 a retrial declared her innocent; she was canonized in 1920.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hand out cards with the seven gifts written on them and ask each student to pick the one they think they need most this month. Keep them for the practice. Take a quick show of hands for each gift.",
    teach=["Read Isaiah 11:1-3. Say: “‘The Spirit of the Lord shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and the fear of the Lord.’ This is the source of the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit. They are permanent dispositions that make us ready to follow the Spirit’s promptings. We receive them in Baptism, and they are strengthened in Confirmation. Jesus had them in fullness.”", "Say: “The seven gifts: Wisdom lets us see all things in relation to God and his plan. Understanding helps us grasp the truths of faith. Counsel helps us choose rightly in hard decisions. Fortitude (courage) gives strength to do good despite fear. Knowledge helps us see created things as they relate to God. Piety (reverence) is love and devotion toward God as Father. Fear of the Lord is awe and wonder before God, and a sorrow at offending him. It is not terror but reverence. The gifts perfect the virtues and make us docile to the Spirit.”", "Read Galatians 5:22-23. Say: “The fruits of the Spirit are the results when the Spirit works in us: ‘love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control.’ The Church’s traditional list has twelve: charity, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, generosity, gentleness, faithfulness, modesty, self-control, chastity. The gifts are like the root system, the fruits are what we can see. You can tell a tree by its fruit. Ask: What fruit do others see in me?”"],
    ask=["Name the seven gifts. (Wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety, fear of the Lord.)", "What is fear of the Lord? (Awe and reverence, not terror.)", "What is the difference between gifts and fruits? (Gifts are dispositions; fruits are the results we show.)"],
    practice=["Make a card for each gift.", "On each, write a short definition, a moment from Joan’s life or another saint’s, and a way you might need it this week.", "Underline the one you most need.", "Pray for it with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Come, Holy Spirit, give me your seven gifts.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray each day for one gift.”")),

'8-6-3': dict(
  saint="Cyril of Jerusalem: a bishop who explained Confirmation to the newly baptized.",
  tryit=["Walk through the rite of Confirmation.", "Match each sign to its meaning.", "Write your own preparation plan."],
  saintStory="Cyril was born around 313 and was bishop of Jerusalem from about 350. He was exiled from his see three times during the Arian controversies. In Jerusalem, he oversaw the preparation of adults for Baptism at Easter. In Lent he gave lectures, called the catechetical lectures, to the candidates on the Creed. During the week after Easter, he gave the newly baptized a series of talks, called mystagogical catecheses, explaining the sacraments they had received.\nHe described the anointing after Baptism: “As Christ after his baptism and the coming of the Holy Spirit went out and conquered the adversary, so you also, after holy Baptism and the sacramental chrism, put on the whole armor of the Holy Spirit.” He explained that the oil is no mere ointment, but the gift of Christ and the Holy Spirit, and that the forehead, ears, nostrils and breast are anointed.\nHe died in 386 and is a Doctor of the Church. His talks give a window into how the early Church celebrated these sacraments.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Why do athletes and soldiers get a uniform or special oil or mark before they begin? What does it signal?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Read Acts 8:14-17 and 19:1-6. Say: “In Samaria, those baptized by Philip did not yet receive the Spirit until Peter and John arrived and laid hands on them. In Ephesus, Paul laid hands on baptized disciples, and the Holy Spirit came on them. From apostolic times, the Church has joined Baptism with a laying on of hands and prayer for the Spirit. This is the sacrament of Confirmation. Together with Baptism and the Eucharist, it is one of the sacraments of Christian initiation.”", "Say: “In the Latin Church, the bishop is the ordinary minister of Confirmation, though priests can confirm with permission (for example, at the Easter Vigil for adults). In the Eastern Churches, the priest confirms (called chrismation) right after Baptism, even for infants. The essential rite has two parts: the laying on of hands (an extended gesture over all the candidates) and the anointing of the forehead with sacred chrism, with the words ‘Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.’ The chrism is blessed by the bishop at the Chrism Mass during Holy Week.”", "Say: “What does it do? It increases and deepens the grace of Baptism. It gives a special strength of the Holy Spirit to spread and defend the faith by word and deed. It seals the person with an indelible mark, so it is only received once. A candidate needs to be baptized, in the state of grace, prepared and instructed, and choose a sponsor, who should be a practicing Catholic, to accompany them. Many choose a Confirmation name, a saint to be a patron. Confirmation is not graduation from the Church. It is the beginning of mission.”"],
    ask=["Who usually confirms? (The bishop.)", "What are the two parts of the rite? (Laying on of hands and anointing with chrism.)", "What do the words say? (Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.)"],
    practice=["Number cards: Presentation of candidates, Homily, Renewal of baptismal promises, Laying on of hands, Anointing with chrism, Sign of peace, Prayer of the faithful.", "Put them in order.", "Beside each, write what it means.", "Write a preparation plan: which saint you’ll choose, who your sponsor is, a service project, and a promise to pray."],
    close="Pray: “Send forth your Spirit, and they shall be created, and you shall renew the face of the earth.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Talk with your sponsor or a parent about your Confirmation.”")),

'8-7-1': dict(
  saint="Blessed Franz Jägerstätter: an Austrian farmer who followed his conscience against Hitler.",
  tryit=["Work through the steps of forming conscience.", "Apply them to a real dilemma.", "Write one habit to keep."],
  saintStory="Franz Jägerstätter was born in 1907 in a small village in Austria, the son of an unmarried farm maid, and raised by his grandmother. As a young man, he had a wild streak, but after marrying Franziska in 1936 and going on a honeymoon pilgrimage to Rome, he grew deeply devout. He became the village sexton, and prayed daily.\nWhen Germany annexed Austria in 1938, he was the only person in his village to vote against it. In 1943, he was called up for military service. He believed that the war was unjust, and that he could not take the oath to Hitler. His pastor and bishop urged him to think of his family. His wife supported him. “I cannot and may not take an oath in favor of a government that is fighting an unjust war.”\nHe was arrested, tried and sentenced to death. He was beheaded on August 9, 1943, at thirty-six. He was beatified in 2007, and his story has become a model of conscience.",
  script=dict(
    open="Read this case aloud: a friend says, ‘My conscience is clear, so it is fine.’ Ask, “Is a clear conscience always a right conscience?” Let two students argue each side for one minute.",
    teach=["Read Romans 2:14-16. Say: “Paul writes that the law is ‘written on their hearts, while their conscience also bears witness.’ Conscience is the judgment of reason by which we recognize the moral quality of an act, past or about to be done. The Catechism says it is ‘a judgment of reason whereby the human person recognizes the moral quality of a concrete act.’ Vatican II called conscience ‘the most secret core and sanctuary of a man, where he is alone with God.’ We must always follow our conscience, and we must always form it.”", "Say: “Conscience can be mistaken. It must be formed by Scripture, the teaching of the Church, prayer, advice from wise people and examination. A poorly formed conscience can be culpable if the ignorance is our fault. Jägerstätter’s conscience was well formed: he read the Bible and the teaching of the Church, prayed, and asked for advice. How do we form conscience? Study the Gospel and the Ten Commandments. Learn the Church’s teaching (the Catechism). Pray for light. Ask for advice. Examine the day. Avoid occasions of sin. And be honest with yourself.”", "Say: “Freedom is not doing whatever we want, but the power to choose the good. The more we do good, the freer we become; sin enslaves. Jesus said, ‘The truth will make you free.’ (John 8:32) We must always do what we judge to be right, and never do something we think is wrong. A conscience can be certain, doubtful or erroneous. If doubtful, we must seek to clarify. We are responsible for our acts. Three sources of a moral act: the object (what we do), the intention (why we do it), and the circumstances. An act is good only if all three are good. A good end does not justify a bad means.”"],
    ask=["What is conscience? (A judgment of reason about the goodness of an act.)", "How do we form conscience? (Scripture, Church teaching, prayer, advice, examination.)", "What are the three sources of morality? (Object, intention, circumstances.)"],
    practice=["Give a dilemma: a friend asks you to copy your homework; a group wants to post a mean joke about a classmate; you find a wallet with cash.", "Work through the steps: What is the act? What is my intention? What are the circumstances? What does the Church teach? Who could I ask?", "Decide what to do.", "Write one habit of conscience-formation you will start."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, give me a well-formed conscience and the courage to follow it.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Do a short examination of conscience each night.”")),

'8-7-2': dict(
  saint="John Vianney: a parish priest who became a saint through patience, fasting and hours in the confessional.",
  tryit=["Sort acts and habits into virtues and vices.", "Name the capital sin behind each.", "Choose one virtue to practice."],
  saintStory="Jean-Marie Vianney was born in 1786 in France, in a farming family, during the French Revolution, when Mass was often illegal. He struggled in school, especially with Latin, and nearly failed to be ordained. When he was finally ordained at twenty-nine, he was sent to Ars, a tiny village of about 230 people that had grown cold in the faith.\nHe lived very simply, ate little, often just boiled potatoes, and slept little. He visited every family, taught the children and spent many hours in prayer. People started to attend Mass, and the dances and taverns emptied. Word spread, and eventually 20,000 pilgrims a year came to Ars to go to confession to him. He spent sixteen to eighteen hours a day in the confessional.\nHe died in 1859, and is patron of parish priests. He taught: “The sinner does not have to be pursued; God’s mercy is waiting.”",
  script=dict(
    open="Write ‘habit’ on the board and have students list three habits they have that help them and three that hurt them. Ask, “How did you build these? Can you undo them?” Collect a few.",
    teach=["Say: “A virtue is a habit of doing good. It is a firm disposition we build by practice, with God’s help. There are human virtues, which we acquire by effort, and theological virtues, which God gives. The four cardinal virtues are the hinges on which the others turn: Prudence: knowing the right thing to do. Justice: giving each what is due. Fortitude: courage in difficulty. Temperance: moderation in pleasure. The three theological virtues are faith, hope and charity (love), which are infused by God and have God for their object. Paul wrote, ‘So faith, hope, love abide, these three; but the greatest of these is love.’”", "Say: “The opposite of virtue is vice, a habit of doing wrong. Sin is a failure to love. We have already learned that sin may be mortal or venial. The Church also speaks of the seven capital sins, those which lead to other sins: pride, avarice (greed), envy, wrath (anger), lust, gluttony, sloth. Each has a corresponding virtue: pride and humility, greed and generosity, envy and kindness, anger and patience, lust and chastity, gluttony and temperance, sloth and diligence. Pride is the root of the others.”", "Say: “How do we grow in virtue? By practice: every time we choose the good, it gets easier. By grace in the sacraments and prayer. By avoiding occasions of sin. By having a daily examination of conscience. By having good friends and role models. Ben Franklin made a chart for virtues; so can you. Also: sin is not only what we do, but what we fail to do (sins of omission). And we can share in the sins of others by encouraging them. We resist temptation as Jesus did, and we use the sacrament of Confession to be restored.”"],
    ask=["Name the four cardinal virtues. (Prudence, justice, fortitude, temperance.)", "Name the three theological virtues. (Faith, hope, charity.)", "How many capital sins are there? (Seven.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards of actions: sharing lunch, gossip, finishing homework, angry outburst, honesty on a test, skipping Mass.", "Sort each into a virtue or vice, naming which one.", "Match the vices to the capital sins, and the corresponding virtue.", "Choose a virtue to practice this week, and plan a daily tracking mark."],
    close="Pray the Act of Contrition. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Track your virtue each day.”")),

'8-7-3': dict(
  saint="Damien of Molokai: a priest who lived with those with leprosy and became one of them.",
  tryit=["List the situations where human dignity is at stake.", "Apply the principle of dignity.", "Plan one act of respect."],
  saintStory="Jozef De Veuster was born in Belgium in 1840, the son of a farmer, and became a priest of the Sacred Hearts, taking the name Damien. He was sent to Hawaii in 1864. In 1873, he volunteered to go to Molokai, an isolated peninsula where people with leprosy, then called Hansen’s disease, were sent, mostly to die. Conditions were terrible: little food, shelter or order.\nDamien built houses, a church and a water system; he dressed wounds, dug graves and built coffins. He did not keep away. He shared meals with the sick, and wrote, “I make myself a leper with the lepers to gain all to Jesus Christ.” One day in 1885, while soaking his feet in very hot water, he felt nothing. He realized that he had caught the disease. In his next sermon, he began, “We lepers…”\nDamien died in 1889, at forty-nine. Mahatma Gandhi later said that he was one of the heroes of humanity. He was named a saint in 2009.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Who do people in our society treat as if they matter less? Who do they ignore?” Let students answer; list on the board. “These are the people the Church says have equal dignity.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that every human being has an inherent dignity because every person is made in God’s image and redeemed by Christ. This dignity does not depend on age, ability, race, wealth or usefulness. It comes from God, and no one can take it away. This is the foundation of Catholic moral teaching. We have seen how the fifth commandment protects life from conception to natural death. But dignity also means how we treat people: with respect, honesty and justice.”", "Say: “Here are areas where dignity is at stake. At the beginning of life: abortion and embryo destruction. At the end: euthanasia and assisted suicide. The Church supports palliative care, which eases suffering, but not killing. In war: just war theory, and the protection of civilians. In society: racism, human trafficking, slavery, abuse of the poor and immigrants. The death penalty: the Catechism says it is ‘inadmissible’ because it is an attack on the inviolability and dignity of the person. Also bullying, hate speech and pornography, which treat persons as objects.”", "Say: “The Church also teaches about the proper care for the body. We must care for our health, avoid addictions (drugs, alcohol abuse), and use medicine for healing. Organ donation can be an act of charity. Medical research is good when it respects life and dignity. Technology, including social media and AI, should serve human persons. The golden rule: ‘Whatever you wish that men would do to you, do so to them.’ (Matthew 7:12) And Jesus’ teaching of the Good Samaritan: our neighbor is anyone in need.”"],
    ask=["Where does human dignity come from? (Being made in God’s image and redeemed by Christ.)", "What does palliative care do? (Eases suffering without killing.)", "Who is my neighbor? (Anyone in need.)"],
    practice=["Brainstorm situations where dignity is at risk (bullying, ignoring a classmate, online humiliation, treatment of the elderly or disabled).", "For each, write what respecting dignity looks like.", "Choose one and plan a specific action for this week.", "Share one."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help me see your image in every person.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Greet someone who is often ignored.”")),

'8-8-1': dict(
  saint="Teresa of Avila: a Carmelite who taught that prayer is friendship with God.",
  tryit=["Try each of the four forms of prayer.", "Write what you noticed.", "Choose one to practice daily."],
  saintStory="Teresa was born in 1515 in Avila, Spain. At twenty, she entered the Carmelite convent of the Incarnation. For almost twenty years, she struggled to pray; she had a lively mind that wandered. The convent was relaxed, with many visitors. Then, standing before a statue of the wounded Christ, she was overcome, and began a deeper life of prayer.\nShe described her experiences and her advice in her books, The Way of Perfection and The Interior Castle. She compared the soul to a castle with many rooms, with God at the center. She said prayer is “nothing else than an intimate sharing between friends; it means taking time frequently to be alone with him who we know loves us.”\nWith John of the Cross, she reformed the Carmelites, founding seventeen convents, traveling over rough roads in a covered cart. She was funny and practical: “From silly devotions and sour-faced saints, good Lord, deliver us!” She died in 1582 and was the first woman declared a Doctor of the Church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say, “Close your eyes for one minute. Notice what your mind does when you try to be silent.” After a minute, ask what they noticed. Say, “This is prayer’s first challenge.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism says that prayer is ‘the raising of one’s mind and heart to God or the requesting of good things from God.’ (CCC 2559) It is a relationship, God’s thirst meeting ours. Prayer is a gift, a covenant and a communion. There are three expressions of prayer: vocal prayer, meditation and contemplation. Vocal prayer uses words, aloud or silently, like the Our Father or the Rosary. Meditation is thinking about God’s word, using the mind and imagination, as in lectio divina. Contemplation is simply being with God, gazing in love, often in silence. Teresa described it as ‘an act of love.’”", "Say: “There are five forms of prayer. Blessing and adoration: we worship God for who he is. Petition: we ask for what we need. Intercession: we ask for others. Thanksgiving: we give thanks. Praise: we glorify God for his own sake. We can remember them with the acronym ACTS or PRAY: Praise, Repent, Ask, Yield. Prayer should include silence. The psalms and the Mass shape our prayers. And we also use sacramentals: a crucifix, an icon, a rosary, holy water.”", "Say: “Prayer can be difficult: distractions, dryness, doubt, being busy, discouragement. Teresa had them for years. Her advice: persevere. Start small, with a fixed time and place. Use a book if you need to. Begin with the Sign of the Cross and a prayer to the Holy Spirit. When distracted, gently return. Don’t expect feelings. Pray even when it feels dry; fidelity matters more than feeling. Jesus said, ‘Ask, and it will be given you.’ (Luke 11:9) The Our Father is the model of Christian prayer.”"],
    ask=["What are the three expressions of prayer? (Vocal, meditation, contemplation.)", "Name the five forms of prayer. (Blessing/adoration, petition, intercession, thanksgiving, praise.)", "What should we do when prayer is dry? (Persevere; fidelity matters more than feelings.)"],
    practice=["Rotate through four stations, three minutes each: vocal prayer (the Our Father slowly), meditation (a Gospel verse), contemplation (silence before a cross), praise (a psalm or hymn).", "At each, write one sentence about what happened.", "Choose the one that helps you most.", "Make a plan for ten minutes a day."],
    close="Pray the Bookmark of Teresa: “Let nothing disturb you, let nothing frighten you; all things pass; God alone is enough.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray ten minutes daily.”")),

'8-8-2': dict(
  saint="Clare of Assisi: a woman who drove away an army by holding up the Blessed Sacrament.",
  tryit=["Spend a quiet time before a monstrance image.", "Write a prayer of adoration.", "Plan a visit to the Blessed Sacrament."],
  saintStory="Clare was born in 1193 in Assisi, to a noble family. At eighteen, she heard Francis of Assisi preach, and was inspired by his poverty. On Palm Sunday night in 1212, she slipped out of her home, met Francis at the Portiuncula chapel, and had her hair cut off. She went to a Benedictine convent, and finally to San Damiano, where she lived in poverty with the other women who joined her, the Poor Clares.\nIn about 1240, according to tradition, an army of Saracen mercenaries was attacking Assisi. They broke into the convent. Clare, sick, asked to be carried to the door, and holding the monstrance with the Blessed Sacrament, she prayed for her sisters. The soldiers fled.\nShe lived at San Damiano for forty years, much of it ill in bed. She died in 1253. She is often shown with a monstrance, and is patron of television, since on her deathbed she saw and heard Mass on the wall of her room, when she was too ill to go to church.",
  script=dict(
    open="Show a picture of a monstrance, if available. Ask, “What is this? Have you ever seen it? What do we do in front of it?”",
    teach=["Say: “The Mass is the center of Catholic life. We have called it the source and summit. Each part of the Mass has its own purpose. The Introductory Rites gather us. The Liturgy of the Word has God speak to us. The Liturgy of the Eucharist unites us to Christ’s sacrifice. The Communion Rite feeds us. The Concluding Rites send us. We should prepare before Mass: fast for one hour, be in the state of grace, arrive early, and pray. We take part actively by responding, singing, listening and offering ourselves with the bread and wine.”", "Say: “The Mass is a sacrifice, a memorial and a banquet. It is the same sacrifice as Calvary. Christ is the priest and the victim. The priest acts in his person. The Mass is not a new sacrifice, but makes the one sacrifice present. We join our lives to it. We are also fed with his Body and Blood. The Eucharist unites us with Christ, with each other, and strengthens us. It forgives venial sins and preserves us from mortal sin.”", "Say: “Outside of Mass, the Blessed Sacrament is reserved in the tabernacle, for Communion to the sick and for adoration. Eucharistic adoration is prayer before the Blessed Sacrament, exposed in a monstrance or in the tabernacle. We kneel, we watch and wait. Jesus asked the apostles at Gethsemane, ‘Could you not watch one hour with me?’ Benediction is a short service in which the priest blesses the people with the Blessed Sacrament. Many saints, like Clare, Thomas Aquinas, Padre Pio and Mother Teresa, drew their strength from adoration. The Eucharistic Procession on Corpus Christi shows our faith publicly.”"],
    ask=["Is the Mass a new sacrifice? (No, it makes present the one sacrifice of Calvary.)", "What is Eucharistic adoration? (Praying before the Blessed Sacrament.)", "What is Benediction? (A blessing with the Blessed Sacrament.)"],
    practice=["Visit the church if you can, or look at a picture of the Blessed Sacrament.", "Be silent for five minutes. Use the prompts: Jesus, I believe you are here. Thank you for… Help me with… I love you.", "Afterward write a prayer of adoration in a few sentences.", "Plan a time this month to visit the Blessed Sacrament."],
    close="Pray the Tantum Ergo or: “Jesus, I adore you.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Visit Jesus in the tabernacle this week.”")),

'8-8-3': dict(
  saint="Charles Lwanga and the Ugandan martyrs: young converts who chose Christ over a king.",
  tryit=["Write your own invitation to faith.", "Practice it in pairs.", "Say what the witness of a life says."],
  saintStory="In the 1880s, the Catholic missionaries known as White Fathers had come to the kingdom of Buganda, in what is now Uganda. A group of young men, pages in the court of King Mwanga II, became Christians. Joseph Mukasa, the head page, asked the king to stop abusing the boys. Mwanga, enraged, had him beheaded in 1885. Charles Lwanga took his place as the leader, and baptized the younger boys that night.\nIn 1886, the king demanded that the Christian pages renounce their faith. They refused. On June 3, twenty-two Catholic men and boys, the youngest about thirteen, were burned alive at Namugongo. They went singing hymns. Another set of Anglican converts were also martyred.\nThe martyrs of Uganda were canonized in 1964. The faith grew rapidly after their deaths. Today Uganda is a heartland of African Catholicism.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a phone with a social media feed. Say, “Everyone shares what they love. What is the best way to share faith so that people hear it and do not just scroll past?” Take suggestions.",
    teach=["Read Matthew 28:18-20. Say: “‘All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to me. Go therefore and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, teaching them to observe all that I have commanded you; and lo, I am with you always, to the close of the age.’ The Church exists to evangelize. This is called the Great Commission. Every baptized person shares in it, not only priests and missionaries.”", "Say: “Pope Paul VI wrote, ‘The Church exists in order to evangelize.’ Pope Francis called all to be ‘missionary disciples.’ Evangelization means proclaiming Jesus Christ, by word and example, and inviting others to a relationship with him. It includes witness of life, which comes first: ‘Let your light so shine before men that they may see your good works and give glory to your Father who is in heaven.’ (Matthew 5:16) Then comes proclamation: explaining the faith. Peter said: ‘Always be prepared to make a defense to anyone who calls you to account for the hope that is in you; yet do it with gentleness and reverence.’ (1 Peter 3:15)”", "Say: “How can we do this in practice? Live your faith joyfully and consistently. Pray for others. Invite a friend to Mass or a youth event. Be ready to share your own story of what Jesus means to you. Know your faith. Treat everyone with respect, listening before talking. Use social media wisely. Serve others. Don’t be pushy or argue just to win. Most people come to faith through a friend. The Holy Spirit does the converting; we are the instruments. And evangelization needs courage, as the Ugandan martyrs showed.”"],
    ask=["What did Jesus command in Matthew 28? (Go, make disciples, baptize and teach.)", "What comes first in evangelization? (Witness of life.)", "Who actually converts hearts? (The Holy Spirit.)"],
    practice=["In pairs, practice a two-minute “faith story”: one thing Jesus means to me.", "Practice responding gently to a friend who says, “Why do you believe?” or “Why go to Mass?”.", "Write a short invitation to a friend for a parish event.", "Pray for the friend by name."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, make me a missionary disciple.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Invite someone to Mass.”")),

'8-9-1': dict(
  saint="Albert the Great: a Dominican who studied rocks, plants and animals to know their Maker.",
  tryit=["Sort the claims into Scripture’s teaching and science’s questions.", "Write what each can and cannot say.", "Summarize the Church’s position."],
  saintStory="Albert was born around 1200 in Germany, to a noble family. He studied at Padua, and joined the Dominicans. He taught in Paris and Cologne, where one of his students was Thomas Aquinas. He was a bishop for a short time, and had to travel on foot because the Dominicans had a rule against riding.\nAlbert was fascinated by the natural world. He observed plants, animals, minerals, stars and weather, and wrote books on them, correcting errors in earlier writings by direct observation. He wrote that the aim of natural science is “not simply to accept what we are told, but to inquire into the causes of natural things.” Because of his wide learning, he was called Doctor Universalis, “Universal Doctor.”\nHe taught that faith and reason are not at odds, and that studying creation leads to its Creator. He died in 1280. In 1941 he was named patron of natural scientists.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Do you think a person can be a scientist and believe in God? Name any you know.” Take answers. Mention Mendel (a monk who founded genetics), Georges Lemaître (priest who proposed the Big Bang) and others.",
    teach=["Say: “Some people think faith and science are enemies. The Church teaches they are not, since truth cannot contradict truth. Pope John Paul II said, ‘Science can purify religion from error and superstition; religion can purify science from idolatry and false absolutes.’ Science asks how and what; faith asks why and who. Science studies the physical world through observation and experiment. Theology studies the meaning of the world as God’s creation and our relation to him. They answer different questions.”", "Say: “Genesis 1-2 is not a scientific description. It teaches religious truths: there is one God who made everything out of nothing; creation is good; humans are made in God’s image, male and female; there is a day of rest; and the world has an order. It uses the symbols and structure of its time. The Church allows that the days may not be literal. St. Augustine, over 1,600 years ago, warned against interpreting Genesis in a way that ‘makes a fool’ of Christians in the eyes of knowledgeable non-believers. The Bible tells us how to go to heaven, not how the heavens go (as Galileo quoted).”", "Say: “The Big Bang theory, proposed by Fr. Georges Lemaître, a Belgian priest and physicist, in 1927, says the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago. This fits with the idea that the universe has a beginning. Pope Pius XII noted that this fits the idea of creation, though science cannot prove God. On evolution, the Church allows that the human body may have developed through evolutionary processes, but each human soul is created directly by God (Humani Generis, 1950). What the Church rejects is the claim that matter alone explains everything, or that humans are accidents. Faith and science can cooperate, with humility on both sides.”"],
    ask=["Can truth contradict truth? (No.)", "What does Genesis 1 teach? (God made everything good, humans in his image, a day of rest.)", "Who proposed the Big Bang? (Fr. Georges Lemaître, a priest.)"],
    practice=["Make two columns: “What science asks” and “What faith asks.”", "Fill in questions: How old is the universe? Why is there something rather than nothing? How did life develop? Why are we here?", "Mark which belongs to each, and where they overlap.", "Write a sentence summarizing the Church’s position."],
    close="Pray Psalm 19:1 or Psalm 8. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Look up a Catholic scientist.”")),

'8-9-2': dict(
  saint="Bartolomé de las Casas: a priest who saw the cruelty of conquest and spent his life defending the Indians.",
  tryit=["Sort claims into fact, myth and debate.", "Check each against the evidence.", "Write what the Church admits and what she denies."],
  saintStory="Bartolomé de las Casas was born in Seville around 1484. He went to the Caribbean in 1502 as a settler, and owned a plantation with native laborers under the encomienda system. He was ordained a priest, the first ordained in the New World. In 1514, while preparing a sermon, he read a passage in Sirach: “Like one who kills a son before his father’s eyes is the man who offers sacrifice from the property of the poor.” He was shaken, freed his laborers, and from then on devoted himself to defending the Indians.\nHe traveled to Spain over a dozen times to petition the king. He wrote A Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies, describing the atrocities. In 1550 he debated in Valladolid with a scholar, Sepúlveda, who argued that native peoples were natural slaves. Las Casas insisted that they were rational human beings with rights.\nHe became a Dominican and bishop of Chiapas. He died in 1566. His record is not perfect, but he is remembered as a defender of human rights.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write three claims on the board: ‘The Church burned Galileo.’ ‘The Crusades were pure greed.’ ‘The Inquisition killed millions.’ Invite students to vote true, partly true, or false. Keep the tally for later.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church is holy but her members are sinners. The Church has done much good, but members have also done wrong, and the Church admits it. In the Jubilee Year 2000, Pope John Paul II asked forgiveness for the sins of Catholics in history. We should look at hard questions honestly, and avoid both myths that the Church is all bad and myths that she is all good. Let’s look at three: Galileo, the Inquisition and the Crusades.”", "Say: “Galileo (1564-1642) argued for the Copernican view that the earth moves around the sun. He was a devout Catholic. Many Church scholars were open to the idea, but the evidence was incomplete, and some interpreted certain Bible verses literally. In 1633 he was tried by the Roman Inquisition and forced to recant, and spent his last years under house arrest. He was not tortured or burned. The affair was a mistake, which John Paul II acknowledged in 1992. But the Church did not oppose science in general: she founded universities, and many scientists were clerics. The Inquisition began in the 1100s to investigate heresy. The Roman Inquisition was fairer than many secular courts, and used limited punishments. The Spanish Inquisition, under the Crown from 1478, was harsher; it executed perhaps a few thousand people over centuries, far fewer than legend suggests. Even so, it was wrong to punish people for beliefs by force.”", "Say: “The Crusades (1095-1291) were military expeditions called by the popes, initially to defend Eastern Christians and to recover Jerusalem, which Muslim armies had conquered, and to protect pilgrims. They were a mix of faith and politics. There were heroic deeds and great sacrifice, but also atrocities, such as the massacre of Jews in the Rhineland and the sack of Jerusalem in 1099, and the sack of Constantinople in 1204 by Western Crusaders, which deeply wounded relations with the East. The Church today does not defend these abuses. Jesus said, ‘Put your sword back.’ History is complex. We should study it honestly, trusting that truth serves the faith.”"],
    ask=["Was Galileo tortured? (No; he was tried and put under house arrest.)", "What did John Paul II do in 2000? (Asked forgiveness for the sins of Church members.)", "Why study history honestly? (Truth serves the faith.)"],
    practice=["Prepare cards of claims: “The Church burned Galileo.” “The Crusades were only about greed.” “The Inquisition killed millions.”", "Sort them into: true, partly true, false.", "Use a short reading or the lesson’s facts as evidence.", "Write one sentence: how I would answer a friend who raised this."],
    close="Pray the prayer of forgiveness: “Lord, forgive the sins of your children, and make us one.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask an adult what they have heard about one of these.”")),

'8-9-3': dict(
  saint="Edith Stein: a philosopher and Carmelite who died at Auschwitz.",
  tryit=["Sort the answers to suffering.", "Mark where Scripture and the Church speak.", "Write a prayer from your own pain."],
  saintStory="Edith Stein was born in 1891 in Breslau, Germany, to a devout Jewish family. As a teenager, she became an atheist. She studied philosophy and became a brilliant assistant to the philosopher Edmund Husserl. During World War I she nursed soldiers. After a friend, a philosopher, was killed in the war, she visited his widow, and was struck by the widow’s faith in the cross. Later, one night in 1921, she read the autobiography of Teresa of Avila, and closed it, saying, “This is the truth.” She was baptized on January 1, 1922.\nShe taught at a Catholic school, then at a college, until the Nazis forced Jews out of teaching positions. In 1933 she entered the Carmelites in Cologne, taking the name Teresa Benedicta of the Cross. In 1938, for her safety, she was moved to a convent in the Netherlands. When the Dutch bishops condemned the persecution of Jews in 1942, the Nazis arrested all Catholic converts of Jewish descent.\nShe was taken to Auschwitz with her sister Rosa, and died in the gas chamber on August 9, 1942. She was canonized in 1998.",
  script=dict(
    open="Be quiet for a moment, then say, “This is the hardest question people ask about God: If God is good and all-powerful, why is there suffering? I want to approach it honestly, because it deserves honesty. Is anyone here comfortable saying they have no pain?”",
    teach=["Say: “The problem of evil is real, and the Church does not pretend there is an easy answer. The Catechism says there is no quick answer; ‘only the Christian message as a whole constitutes the answer.’ (CCC 309) Here are parts of the answer. One: God did not make evil. He made the world good, and gave humans and angels free will. Suffering and death entered through the misuse of freedom, in the fall. Love requires freedom; and freedom can be misused. Two: much suffering results from human sin: war, injustice, cruelty. Natural evils like disease and earthquakes are also consequences of a world that is ‘in a state of journeying’ toward perfection, and of the fall.”", "Say: “Three: God can bring good from evil, as with Joseph: ‘You meant evil against me; but God meant it for good.’ We do not say that God causes evil, but he permits it, and brings greater good from it. Four, and most important: God did not stay at a distance. Jesus entered into our suffering. He wept, he was hungry, he was betrayed, he was tortured and killed. God knows suffering from inside. On the cross, he took our pain upon himself. Five: suffering is not the final word. The Resurrection shows that death is defeated. In the end, God ‘will wipe away every tear from their eyes, and death shall be no more.’ (Revelation 21:4)”", "Say: “The Book of Job asks the question, and God doesn’t explain, but comes to Job and says that his ways are beyond us. Suffering can also be redemptive when united with Christ. Paul said, ‘I rejoice in my sufferings for your sake, and in my flesh I complete what is lacking in Christ’s afflictions.’ (Colossians 1:24) Suffering can draw us closer to God, teach compassion, and purify us. But it is not good in itself, and we must work to relieve it. And we should not hide our pain: the Psalms complain to God. Mother Teresa, Edith Stein and many others found God in the midst of suffering. This does not completely solve the mystery, but it gives hope and strength.”"],
    ask=["Did God make evil? (No; he made the world good and gave freedom.)", "What did Jesus do about suffering? (He entered into it and died for us.)", "What is the final answer? (The Resurrection and God’s promise to wipe away every tear.)"],
    practice=["Write the question at the top: “If God is good, why suffering?”", "Make a list of the answers: free will, God brings good from evil, Jesus shares our suffering, resurrection, redemptive suffering.", "Choose the answer you find most helpful, and write why.", "Write a short honest prayer to God about something hard in your life."],
    close="Pray Psalm 13 or 22, or: “Lord, in my pain, be with me.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Be present to someone who is suffering.”")),

'8-10-1': dict(
  saint="Elizabeth Ann Seton: a widow who lost her fortune and founded the first Catholic parish school in America.",
  tryit=["Sort actions into just and unjust.", "Name the commandment and virtue each touches.", "Plan one act of restitution or generosity."],
  saintStory="Elizabeth Bayley was born in 1774 in New York City, to a prominent family. She married a wealthy merchant, William Seton, and they had five children. Their business failed, and William fell ill with tuberculosis. They sailed to Italy hoping for a cure. He died there, leaving Elizabeth a widow at twenty-nine, with five children, penniless in a foreign land.\nIn Italy, she was cared for by Catholic friends, and became attracted by their faith, especially belief in the Real Presence. She returned to New York and entered the Catholic Church in 1805, which cost her friends and income. A priest invited her to open a school in Baltimore, and she started the Sisters of Charity, the first American religious community. In 1810 they moved to Emmitsburg, Maryland, where she began a school for girls, the start of Catholic parish schools in the United States.\nShe died in 1821, aged forty-six. In 1975 she became the first American-born saint.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up a library book or a borrowed item. Say, “Whose is this? What do you owe the owner?” Then write on the board the different ways people take things that are not theirs, from small to large.",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:15. Say: “‘You shall not steal.’ The seventh commandment protects the right to property and requires respect for others’ goods. It is not only about shoplifting. It forbids theft, robbery, fraud, cheating, breaking contracts, unjust wages, bribery, vandalism, and wasting what belongs to others. It also forbids stealing online: pirating music and software, plagiarism, and copying homework. Ephesians 4:28: ‘Let the thief no longer steal, but rather let him labor, doing honest work with his hands, so that he may be able to give to those in need.’”", "Say: “The Church teaches that God gave the earth to everyone. Private property is right and good, but it has a ‘universal destination’: goods are for the benefit of all. Those with abundance have a duty to share. We must care for creation and not exploit it. We should work honestly, pay just wages, keep promises, and pay our debts. If we have taken what is not ours, we owe restitution: returning it or its worth. Zacchaeus is the model. In the sacrament of Penance, the priest will ask us to make restitution when we can.”", "Say: “Stealing can be subtle. Taking a few minutes of time you are paid for. Not returning a lent item. Cutting corners in work. Not giving a just share to workers. Neglecting the poor: St. John Chrysostom said, ‘Not to enable the poor to share in our goods is to steal from them. The goods we possess are not ours, but theirs.’ Positive virtues: honesty, generosity, justice, simplicity, gratitude. We are stewards of what God gives us. Jesus asked us to give and to lend, expecting nothing in return.”"],
    ask=["What does the seventh commandment forbid? (Theft, fraud, cheating, unjust wages, bribery, damage to property.)", "What is restitution? (Returning what was taken, or its value.)", "What does the universal destination of goods mean? (God gave the earth for the benefit of all.)"],
    practice=["Prepare scenarios: finding $20, copying a friend’s essay, keeping an overpaid change, a download without paying, paying a babysitter less than promised.", "For each, decide: just or unjust? What virtue or commandment applies? What should you do?", "Choose a past action you might need to repair.", "Plan a restitution or generous act."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, teach me to be honest and generous.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Return something you borrowed.”")),

'8-10-2': dict(
  saint="Polycarp: a bishop who would not lie, even to save his life.",
  tryit=["Sort statements into truth, lie, gossip, calumny and white lie.", "Say what each does to trust.", "Write a rule for your speech."],
  saintStory="Polycarp was bishop of Smyrna (now Izmir, Turkey) and a disciple of the apostle John. He was one of the Apostolic Fathers. Ignatius of Antioch wrote him a letter on his way to martyrdom. Polycarp wrote a letter to the Philippians, and he visited Rome to talk with Pope Anicetus about the date of Easter.\nIn about the year 155, when Polycarp was an old man, a persecution broke out in Smyrna. He could have escaped, but was arrested. The proconsul urged him to swear by the emperor’s fortune and curse Christ. “Eighty-six years I have served him,” Polycarp said, “and he has done me no wrong. How can I blaspheme my King who saved me?”\nHe was burned at the stake, and prayed aloud, giving thanks that he had been counted worthy to be numbered among the martyrs. The account of his death is one of the earliest martyrdom accounts, written by his own community.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Have you ever told a small lie to be polite or to avoid trouble? Did it cause any problem? What would the world be like if no one could trust what anyone said?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:16. Say: “‘You shall not bear false witness against your neighbor.’ The eighth commandment protects truth and trust. It originally referred to false testimony in court, but it covers all lying. Jesus is ‘the truth’ (John 14:6), and the devil is the ‘father of lies’ (John 8:44). Lying means speaking a falsehood with the intent to deceive. Lies destroy trust. A lie is always wrong, even a ‘white lie’ to be polite or to avoid trouble. We can be tactful without lying; and we are not obliged to tell everything.”", "Say: “Other sins against the eighth commandment: perjury (lying under oath); rash judgment (assuming evil about someone without proof); detraction (revealing someone’s faults without a good reason); calumny (telling lies that harm someone’s reputation); gossip; flattery; boasting; and breaking promises. Online: spreading rumors, posting embarrassing images, fake accounts, and plagiarism. If we have damaged someone’s reputation, we must repair it, as far as possible. Secrets: priests keep the seal of confession; professionals keep confidences. But we do not keep secrets that put someone in danger. We should tell a trusted adult.”", "Say: “The positive side: truthfulness, honesty and integrity. ‘Let what you say be simply Yes or No.’ (Matthew 5:37) We give others the benefit of the doubt. We speak the truth in love (Ephesians 4:15). We use media wisely: seeking facts, and not sharing what we cannot verify. And we bear witness to Christ by our words and lives, even at cost. The martyrs are the ultimate witnesses to truth. Polycarp could have told a small lie, but he would not. Trust is built over years and can be lost in a moment.”"],
    ask=["What is a lie? (Speaking a falsehood with intent to deceive.)", "What is detraction? (Revealing someone’s faults without good reason.)", "What is calumny? (Lies that harm someone’s reputation.)"],
    practice=["Write statements on cards: “I didn’t do my homework because I was sick,” “I heard she…,” “Nice haircut” (when you dislike it), “I can’t go; I have plans,” “He cheated, but I won’t say.”", "Sort into: truthful, a lie, gossip, calumny, tact.", "Discuss tricky cases: what honest, kind responses would you give?", "Write a personal rule for speech."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, set a guard over my mouth.” (Psalm 141:3.) Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Go one day without gossip.”")),

'8-10-3': dict(
  saint="Francis of Assisi: a rich young man who gave up everything and found he was free.",
  tryit=["Make a list of what you want and what you need.", "Find the root of each want.", "Choose one thing to give away."],
  saintStory="Francis was born around 1181 in Assisi, the son of a wealthy cloth merchant. He was charming, fun-loving and wanted to be a knight. After being captured in a war and falling ill, he began to wonder what his life was for. One day, he met a leper on the road, and, though revolted, got down from his horse and kissed his hand. Soon after, in the ruined chapel of San Damiano, he heard the crucifix say, “Francis, repair my house.”\nHe took cloth from his father’s shop and sold it to repair the chapel. His angry father took him to the bishop of Assisi. Francis took off his clothes, handed them to his father, and said, “Until now I have called you my father on earth; now I can say freely, ‘Our Father who art in heaven.’” He chose a life of poverty, preaching and serving the poor.\nOthers joined him, and the Franciscans were founded. Francis sang about “Brother Sun and Sister Moon.” He died in 1226, naked on the ground, at peace.",
  script=dict(
    open="Display a fashion ad or a new-phone ad. Ask, “What does this promise? What feeling is it selling?” Take answers and write them down. Say, “Let’s test whether the promise is true.”",
    teach=["Read Exodus 20:17 and Luke 12:15. Say: “‘You shall not covet your neighbor’s house… or anything that is your neighbor’s.’ The tenth commandment goes to the heart. The seventh forbids taking; the tenth forbids even wanting what belongs to another in a way that leads to envy or greed. Jesus said, ‘Take heed, and beware of all covetousness; for a man’s life does not consist in the abundance of his possessions.’ Coveting is the root of many sins. It is a disordered desire that makes us unhappy.”", "Say: “Greed is the desire for more and more. Envy is sorrow at another’s good and wanting it for ourselves; it is one of the capital sins. In the Bible, Cain envied Abel, Joseph’s brothers envied him, and Saul envied David. Social media can feed envy, since we see only others’ best moments. The remedy is gratitude, humility and generosity. Detachment from possessions: Jesus said, ‘Blessed are the poor in spirit.’ Paul wrote, ‘I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content… I can do all things in him who strengthens me.’ (Philippians 4:11-13)”", "Say: “Poverty of spirit does not mean we must all be poor. It means we don’t cling to things, we use them with gratitude and share them. Simplicity brings freedom. Francis and Clare showed that. The Church also asks us to guard the heart from impure desires, the ninth commandment. All of these commandments concern the heart: ‘Create in me a clean heart, O God.’ The goal is to desire what is truly good: God himself. Augustine prayed, ‘Our hearts are restless until they rest in you.’ The best remedy for envy is to rejoice in another’s good, and thank God for our own.”"],
    ask=["What does the tenth commandment forbid? (Envy and greed; disordered desire for others’ goods.)", "What is the remedy for envy? (Gratitude, humility, rejoicing in others’ good.)", "What does ‘poor in spirit’ mean? (Not clinging to possessions; trusting God.)"],
    practice=["Make two lists: things you want, and things you need.", "Beside each “want,” ask: Where did this want come from (an ad, a friend, a post)?", "Choose one thing you can give away or give up, and a person who needs it.", "Write three things you are grateful for."],
    close="Pray the Prayer of Saint Francis: “Lord, make me an instrument of your peace.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Give away something this week.”")),
}
